      \field{abstract}{In graph theory, the degree (or valency) of a vertex of a graph is the number of edges that are incident to the vertex; in a multigraph, a loop contributes 2 to a vertex's degree, for the two ends of the edge. The degree of a vertex v \{\textbackslash displaystyle v\} is denoted deg ⁡ ( v ) \{\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash deg(v)\} or deg ⁡ v \{\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash deg v\} . The maximum degree of a graph G \{\textbackslash displaystyle G\} is denoted by Δ ( G ) \{\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash Delta (G)\} , and is the maximum of G \{\textbackslash displaystyle G\} 's vertices' degrees. The minimum degree of a graph is denoted by δ ( G ) \{\textbackslash displaystyle \textbackslash delta (G)\} , and is the minimum of G \{\textbackslash displaystyle G\} 's vertices' degrees. In the multigraph shown on the right, the maximum degree is 5 and the minimum degree is 0. In a regular graph, every vertex has the same degree, and so we can speak of the degree of the graph. A complete graph (denoted K n \{\textbackslash displaystyle K\_\{n\}\} , where n \{\textbackslash displaystyle n\} is the number of vertices in the graph) is a special kind of regular graph where all vertices have the maximum possible degree, n − 1 \{\textbackslash displaystyle n-1\} . In a signed graph, the number of positive edges connected to a vertex is called its positive degree and the number of connected negative edges is called negative degree.}
